@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.1899 = private unnamed_addr constant [900 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `sys.version[0]`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf the current major or minor version consists of multiple digits,\0A`sys.version[0]` will select the first digit of the major version number\0Aonly (e.g., `\2210.2\22` would evaluate to `\221\22`). This is likely unintended,\0Aand can lead to subtle bugs if the version string is used to test against a\0Amajor version number.\0A\0AInstead, use `sys.version_info.major` to access the current major version\0Anumber.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version[0]  # If using Python 10, this evaluates to \221\22.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Af\22{sys.version_info.major}\22  # If using Python 10, this evaluates to \2210\22.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
